Test file: src/claim-office.spec.ts
Implementation file: src/claim-office.ts
Stack profile: .claude/rules/tdd_with_ts_and_vitest.md (TypeScript + Vitest; run `pnpm test:unit:basic`)

Failing test: "should reject a claim containing a damage entry with a negative amount"
  (in describe "MHPCO Claim Office" > "Claim: errors")

It asserts a THROW:
  expect(() =>
    runScenario({
      customer: {yearsWithMHPCO: 0},
      steps: [
        {op: "quote", items: [{type:"sword", material:"steel", enchantment:3, cursed:false}]},
        {op: "claim", policy: 0, incident: {cause:"fire", damages: [{itemType:"sword", amount:-200}]}},
      ],
    }),
  ).toThrow();

Current error (verified by running `pnpm test:unit:basic`):
  AssertionError: expected [Function] to throw an error
  at src/claim-office.spec.ts:1061:9
  (it returns normally with {payout: -300, remainingCap: 2300} — a NEGATIVE payout that also RAISES the cap above its original 2000)

Counts: 1 failed | 48 passed | 6 todo (55). `npx tsc --noEmit` exits 0. `npx eslint src --ext .ts` exits 0.

SPEC (prompt.md lines 179-181): "claim contains a damage entry with `amount: -200` → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code and writes an error description to stderr".

WHERE THIS BELONGS (verified current text). `claimOutcome` opens with a pre-arithmetic guard:

  const rejectOvercountedDamages = (incident: Incident, policy: Policy): void => {
    const insured = countByType(policy.items);
    for (const [itemType, damaged] of countDamagesByType(incident.damages)) {
      const covered = insured.get(itemType) ?? 0;
      if (damaged > covered) {
        throw new Error(
          `Claim reports ${damaged} damaged "${itemType}" but the policy covers ${covered}`,
        );
      }
    }
  };

  const claimOutcome = (incident: Incident, policy: Policy): ClaimResult => {
    rejectOvercountedDamages(incident, policy);
    const desired = incident.damages.reduce(
      (total, damage) => total + payoutForDamage(damage, policy.items), 0);
    const payout = roundPayout(reducedToRemainingCap(desired, policy.remainingCap));
    return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };
  };

That guard's doc comment states the principle: "The whole claim is rejected, so this runs before any payout is computed and before the cap is touched — a policy must not be partially drawn down by a claim that is about to be refused."

A negative amount is the same kind of rule: a whole-claim rejection, not a per-damage adjustment. So add a SECOND guard alongside the first — a separate named function called before the arithmetic — rather than clamping inside `payoutForDamage`. Do NOT fold it into `rejectOvercountedDamages`; the two express different rules and a later spec change would touch them independently.

MINIMAL CHANGE: a guard that throws when any damage entry has a negative amount. Name it in the established style (the existing guard is `rejectOvercountedDamages`). Message should name the offending value and item type, matching the existing message's level of detail — these reach stderr via the CLI.

A JUDGEMENT CALL, yours to make: is ZERO a valid damage amount? The spec says nothing about `amount: 0`, and no active or inactive test covers it. Reject only what the spec rejects — `amount: -200` is negative. A zero-damage entry would pay `0 - 100 = -100` before clipping, which is arguably as nonsensical, but that is NOT what this test demands and no test pins it. Prefer rejecting only negatives; if you disagree, say so in your report rather than silently widening the rule.

DO NOT implement CLI behaviour (exit codes, stderr, stdin parsing) — `src/cli.ts` does not exist yet and has its own two todos.

REGRESSION RISK — every claim test flows through `claimOutcome`. All existing damage amounts are positive (200, 300, 500, 800, 901, 1000, 1401, 1500, 3001), so a negatives-only guard must not disturb them. Verify especially: rune dmg 200 -> 100; sword 500 + amulet 300 -> 600; cap exhaustion 1400/600 then 600/0; ench9 dmg 901 -> 350.

LINT TRAPS: `no-magic-numbers` is `{ignore:[0,1,-1], ignoreArrayIndexes:true}` — note 0 and -1 ARE in the ignore list, so a `< 0` comparison is fine. `sonarjs/cognitive-complexity` limit 10. `max-params` limit 4. Verify eslint's REAL exit code (do not pipe through `tail`).

After the change run `pnpm test:unit:basic` and confirm 49 passed | 6 todo with NO regression. Keep `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx eslint src --ext .ts` at exit 0.

EXPERIMENT MODE: run autonomously, no human approval needed.

